/* book Worked example: Q: Steam enters the turbine of an ideal Rankine cycle at 40 bar and 400 °C and the condenser pressure is 0.1 bar. Find the dryness at the turbine exit, the turbine and pump work, the heat added, the cycle efficiency and the specific steam consumption. Compare the efficiency with a Carnot cycle between the same extreme temperatures.
   1. Steam tables at 40 bar, 400 °C (superheated): h₁ = 3214.5 kJ/kg, s₁ = 6.7714 kJ/kg·K.
   2. At 0.1 bar (Tsat = 45.81 °C): hf = 191.81, hfg = 2392.1 kJ/kg; sf = 0.6492, sfg = 7.4996, sg = 8.1488 kJ/kg·K; vf = 0.001010 m³/kg.
   3. s₁ = 6.7714 < sg = 8.1488, so the exhaust is wet: x₂ = (6.7714 − 0.6492)/7.4996 = 0.8163; h₂ = 191.81 + 0.8163 × 2392.1 = 2144.5 kJ/kg.
   4. wT = 3214.5 − 2144.5 = 1070.0 kJ/kg.
   5. h₃ = hf = 191.81 kJ/kg. wP = 0.001010 × (4000 − 10) = 4.03 kJ/kg (pressures in kPa); h₄ = 195.84 kJ/kg.
   6. qin = 3214.5 − 195.84 = 3018.7 kJ/kg; wnet = 1070.0 − 4.03 = 1066.0 kJ/kg.
   7. η = 1066.0/3018.7 = 0.353. SSC = 3600/1066.0 = 3.38 kg/kWh.
   8. Carnot between 673.15 K and 318.96 K: 1 − 318.96/673.15 = 0.526. The Rankine cycle is lower because its mean temperature of heat addition is only qin/(s₁ − s₄) = 3018.7/(6.7714 − 0.6492) = 493 K (s₄ = s₃ = sf), and 1 − 318.96/493 = 0.353.
   Answer: x₂ = 0.816, wT = 1070.0 kJ/kg, wP = 4.03 kJ/kg, qin = 3018.7 kJ/kg, η = 35.3%, SSC = 3.38 kg/kWh; Carnot 52.6%. */
