/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - In the reheat cycle the states are: 1 high-pressure (HP) turbine inlet, 2 HP turbine exit, 3 reheater exit (low-pressure turbine inlet), 4 LP turbine exit, 5 condenser exit, 6 pump exit.
   - The main gain is dryness: the LP expansion starts at a higher entropy, so the exhaust lies further right.
   - In the regenerative cycle a fraction m of each kg of steam is bled from the turbine and mixed with the feedwater in an open feedwater heater, a mixing chamber at the bleed pressure.
   - The boiler now receives water at about the saturation temperature of the bleed pressure instead of the condenser temperature.
   - A closed feedwater heater is a shell-and-tube exchanger: the feedwater passes through tubes and the bled steam condenses outside them, so the two streams do not mix and can be at different pressures.
*/
