/* book Worked example 2: Q: The same plant (60 bar, 500 °C, 0.1 bar, no reheat) bleeds steam at 5 bar to one open feedwater heater. The water leaves the heater as saturated liquid at 5 bar. Find the bled fraction, the net work, the heat added and the efficiency.
   1. State 2 (bleed, 5 bar, s = 6.8826): superheated (sg = 6.821 at 5 bar), h₂ = 2774.8 kJ/kg (about 163 °C). State 3 (0.1 bar): h₃ = 2180.0 kJ/kg, from the simple cycle.
   2. State 4: h₄ = 191.81 kJ/kg. Pump 1: 0.001010 × (500 − 10) = 0.49 kJ/kg, so h₅ = 192.30 kJ/kg.
   3. State 6 (saturated liquid at 5 bar, 151.8 °C): h₆ = 640.09 kJ/kg, vf = 0.001093 m³/kg. Pump 2: 0.001093 × (6000 − 500) = 6.01 kJ/kg, so h₇ = 646.10 kJ/kg.
   4. Bled fraction: m = (640.09 − 192.30)/(2774.8 − 192.30) = 447.79/2582.5 = 0.1734.
   5. wT = (3423.1 − 2774.8) + 0.8266 × (2774.8 − 2180.0) = 648.3 + 491.7 = 1140.0 kJ/kg.
   6. wP = 0.8266 × 0.49 + 6.01 = 6.42 kJ/kg; wnet = 1133.6 kJ/kg.
   7. qin = 3423.1 − 646.10 = 2777.0 kJ/kg; η = 1133.6/2777.0 = 0.408.
   Answer: m = 0.173 kg per kg of steam; wnet = 1133.6 kJ/kg; qin = 2777.0 kJ/kg; η = 40.8% against 38.4% for the simple cycle. The work per kg falls by 8%, but the heat added falls by 14%. */
